<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Schlieffen Plan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Schlieffen Plan was the German military plan, named after the chief of the general staff Alfred von Schlieffen, for fighting a two-front war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. It envisaged a rapid sweep through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> to defeat France before turning east. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> profile notes that the plan failed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a> in September 1914, condemning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to prolonged attritional warfare.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/schlieffen-plan.jpg" alt="Schlieffen Plan" width="299" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo studio E. Bieber · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfred_von_Schlieffen_1906.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
